   720	### New canon minted (author's list)
   721	- **The ground at the Stair (flag; one picture for chapters 48–52).** A ridge runs north and south; the Stair road comes south along its top and drops off its western face. Halfway down, the slope eases into a brow, and there the carters' live ruts turn north and go the whole way round a shallow bowl some five hundred yards across. The edge of the Quieting lies forty paces inside the ruts. The old straight road, two grown-over ruts, runs on down from the brow, crosses the edge between two iron bars eight feet apart (the old one on the north side, the newer on the south, with Karis's stone between), and goes to the floor: a hundred and ninety yards of grass, then thirty of floor to the hole. The floor is a bare round plate sixty of Cael's strides across, with no upright thing on it. The hole is two strides square, at the middle; the first step is on its eastern side and the stair goes down westward, forty steps, cut and not laid, every edge unworn; the air changes at the twentieth. The chamber opens westward from the stair's foot: ten strides each way; its roof is the underside of the floor, a foot above Brom's head; dust "as thin as the bloom on a plum". The rim (the ruts) is two hundred and sixty yards from the hole and sixteen hundred paces and a little more round.
   722	- **The chamber (flag; changed in repair r1):** ten strides square, as paced on the 115th; the roof is the underside of the floor above, without beam or crack, and stands **a hand above Brom's head** (Cael had called it a foot on the 114th, at a crouch; Brom's palm lies on it with the elbow bent). Dust as thin as the bloom on a plum. **The walls** are cut from floor to roof in five courses, each as tall as a forearm, in the same hand as the bands on the Fallow's stones (Karis can see the bars). **There is no groove.** What Karis keeps for the last of the oil is the protected finding: a line that is not on the Ring, on every wall, not in the courses but *between* them, on its own, recurring "every ten cords and a piece. Two hundred and twenty yards and a stride, to the stride… at the perimeter's radius." How that spacing is read off walls ten strides long is not glossed on the page (repair flag 2). She samples the walls on twenty-nine sheets, and twice walks the foot of a wall with the short cord, counting.
   723	- **The stair's air (changed in repair r1):** from the twentieth step down the air does not move; a lamp flame stands "like a nail" (Brom sees it in Karis's lamp before anybody names the step; Seln: "The air stops there."). In the chamber it is colder than the frost above and "smells of nothing" (Oryn). The gate bow's eighth ranging bolt lies on the thirty-first step until Lira takes it up on the 115th.
   724	- **Hound sign at the Stair (flag):** old runs in the grass on the north side, unused since the frosts began; no den that Seln can find from outside. He says that he does not know why and will not guess. No stillhound appears in this movement.
   725	- **The six on the rim (flag).** Hooded, in road grey, each with a carter's dust-cloth across the face; no word, cry or call from any of them at any point. Four stations outside the edge: at the gate (the brow), *the gate bow* and, two yards south of him, *the empty-handed one*; on the south, *the south bow* and *the standing one*, who carries nothing and gives one sign at the end (an arm raised and let fall); *the west bow*, alone, who never looses; *the north bow*, alone. **Four crossbows among six people.** They lay up off the ruts until the five were over the stone (Seln walked the ruts at dusk and before light and found nothing on them).
   726	- **The second Anchor hand (flag):** the empty-handed one at the gate. The course is three lines, each as thick as a wrist, at the knee, the chest and over a tall man's head, thrown from forty paces across the old road from the old stake to the newer and on round the edge: "the same trade in a different hand… this hand threw", where the woman on the Ostrand road threaded nineteen fine points from fifteen feet. He keeps his hands inside his sleeves through the day. At the end Cael sees a pair of hands: a man's, broad, short in the finger, with a pale band where a ring was taken off before the work. Nothing else of him.
   727	- **The knot (flag):** a single point as big as a fist, a hand above the turf, on the outer face of the **old** stake; three courses come into it from the south and three from the north (the two ends of one ring), and nothing comes out. Cael first looks for it on the newer stake, where the lines landed: "A rope is not made fast where it lands. It is made fast where the hauling starts." He lies in the north rut, reaches round the bar and lays his palm on it. How far round the edge the course ran is not seen; he feels "a great deal of it" run out.
   728	- **The rim's bolts (flag): thirteen.** Gate bow eleven (Brom's shoulder; one during the run; eight ranging shots at the hole, the eighth down the stair to the thirty-first step; one at Cael outside the stone at dusk, which misses); north bow one and south bow one, lofted during the run; west bow none. They are too stout for the channel of the healer's bow (Brom tries one along the stock). On the 115th the boy lays the recovered shafts in a row by the four bows, Lira adds the one from the stair, and the head cut from Brom's bolt lies by the stone; the figure thirteen is given once, in the night log of the 114th. Teague reads eleven stirrup-prints "and a twelfth half made" on the brow.
   729	- **How the six leave (flag):** each bow has its string cut and the nut drawn from its lock, and is laid down on its side; the maker's stamp has been filed off the stock and the place oiled; no armoury number. Seln: "bought for one day, by somebody who meant from the first to leave it lying." Nothing else is left at any station. The four bows lie in a row by the stone on the 115th, one in two pieces.
   730	- **The boxes were not touched** by the six: Seln had laid a stem of grass under every knot. His reason: six people with a quarter of a mile of rim apiece do not leave it.
   731	- **Karis's leaf:** on the night of the 113th she takes the leaf of the trials (with the struck sixth row) out of the middle box and carries it inside her coat.
   732	- **The second paper's history (flag):** written at one desk and one sitting with the first (the same pricked margin, from a wheel with a tooth missing, so that every eleventh hole stands wide; the same fault in a letter's foot); sealed inside the same wrapper, with three hundred counted in a bag and a word of direction that it was not to be posted until the first was answered; kept in the broker's strong-box, he not knowing what it was; brought up by the clerk on the morning after the Fallow line was said; said twice to the meadow, and nobody scratched; read by Teague, who hung it on the nail himself, eight days before the 112th. It carries the hook and the bar.
   733	- **The nail (flag):** a long nail in the plaster by the lower corner of the wall-sheet, where a slip is hung that a captain has taken down, read through and handed back, "so that the next captain knows it has been weighed and by whom". There is hardly ever anything on it.
   734	- **The wall on the 112th:** the five are eleventh; the crew of four with the wooden foot is over them again. Pike: "The cloth can't say whose fault a thing was. It can only say what was on the cart."
   735	- **Pike's HOLD on his own mark:** "If they're late down, nobody comes to me for that slip. It stays theirs till I've seen them or seen where they stopped." He has said yes to one man in thirty years.
   736	- **Brom's father and the horse (flag):** the year Brom was nine, a grey of sixteen hands, worth three hundred, bought at a fair for a hundred from a dealer who would not say why; it went over backward on a hard road, broke his father's collarbone and a groom's leg. "The dealer had priced that horse to a penny. It was my father who read the figure as luck."
   737	- **Rooke's line, as Brom tells it:** said in his ear in his first season, when he had a second-year shut into the corner of the frames with his own back to the gap.
   738	- **Seln's window:** the clerk is no longer young (his capitals are of a pattern not taught for twenty years); there were two people in that room, a hand that wrote and a voice that knew the ground. He weighs walking south alone with the case before light, and does not.
   739	- **Millrace (flag):** an hour below the fork, in a narrow green valley; fast brown water over a weir of stakes and stone; a race along the bank to a tall grey mill with a wheel as high as a barn door; a fence of split posts and a dozen roofs; a ford below, with a flat stone on each bank; four chimneys. The ford-keeper is a woman who sits on the nearer stone with a net across her knees and counts people off the ridge. The healer's place is a lean-to of three board walls and a turf roof against the mill's southern wall, with a plank on two kegs and a bench along the wall. The mill's carter is a round floured man; a second carter, long and stooped, takes her word north.
   740	- **The fifteen:** a miller's man whose right hand went between the hoist's rope and its drum nine days before; a girl of three or four with a rattling chest, low and on the one side (not the child of the far gate); an old woman, small and upright, carried down the valley on a hurdle by two grandsons, who went through the fourth rung of a ladder getting a ham down ("It's got a ladder in it."); and a dozen with one hand each. Three mendings: "That's the day."
   741	- **Her sum on the 113th (flag):** due at Millrace on the 96th, in on the 112th: sixteen. Seventeen at the ford with the oxen, on the 121st. A half-day up, a day, a half-day down: nineteen at the ford, and the child not heard before the 128th. **Her word north:** late, nineteen "or more", at the edge of bad ground a half-day south of the mill; if the child's breathing changes, send down the water to the mill, and the mill sends up the ridge.
   742	- **Lira's bowls (flag):** "We lay six bowls… Brom puts six out every night and takes one back." / "I'll eat out of it. I'll not sit in a place." At the Stair she eats out of the last of the six, standing.
   743	- **The mill's jenny (flag):** mouse-grey, enormous in the belly, ears like oars; she leads "as a gate swings", comes up the notch backward, and brays at every dusk. The miller will thank Oryn to take her "as far as the snow". Oryn does not touch the brown mule.
   744	- **The tenth on another person (flag; changed in repair r1).** He asks twice on the 113th, and they are two questions: at Millrace he asks the healer, at her own bench, with her line waiting, and is refused; on the road she offers, as "a woman on a rock" who can say yes (her protected line: "the same day as the lean-to and a different question"). **The short reading is done with one hand**, his right on her wrist (inside upward, broad and brown, a white scar across the heel of the hand): the going-out leaves that hand and is back under his palm almost before he has counted; at three there are banks, "close… and high", and by ten he knows the Path "as a friend's step on a stair" is known. For the minute she has him put his left hand on the back of her neck as well: one channel, one thing in it, which does not wander or pool, and leans to meet whatever touches it. Bill to him: the reading hand slow for an hour, and hunger.
   745	- **Wind asked on the stillness:** he does not load on the third count; he asks on "four, and", when the bowman has gone entirely still (the gate-walk's "Shooting's stopping", by way of Lira).
   746	- **Injuries, as fixed on the page.** Brom: a bolt through the back of the right shoulder, a hand below its top, the head out under the collar-bone by a thumb's length; taped round the shaft in the chamber with what was left of Lira's ankle tape; outside the stone Seln cuts the shaft below the head and Oryn draws it; mended in an hour; the arm bound across his chest on the 115th; nothing on that side "till I've said so". Lira: her ankle goes at about the twentieth step and the slung arm takes the wall; the lowest mark of each row opens; bound with a strip of Cael's shirt; closed in a quarter of an hour at the stone; the sling tied shorter, the hand at the collar-bone. Cael: both hands and forearms cold and dead for the evening (Shadow); the right forearm struck (it bends by morning); a weal across the left palm, wound in tape, not mended. Oryn: three mendings on the 113th; two on the night of the 114th ("There isn't a third in me tonight").
   747	- **The 115th.** Teague's four on the rim: Teague on the brow, the spear south, the boy west, the bow north; Teague lends his horn lantern. The pacing runs from the stone northward with the Quieting on the right hand; Lira carries the cord's far end and finds the edge with her foot; Seln cuts the turfs; the old stakes stand within a boot of them; the round is the Fallow's, cord for cord, with the same odd strides.
   748	- **The kit (flag; order changed in repair r1):** found in the middle of the chamber floor. A cord knotted at every fifth span lies run out straight toward the eastern wall for half the room, with the rest of it in a coil that was never paid out ("measuring from the middle of that room to its wall, and had got half way"); then a mallet with an iron-bound head; a bar numbered thirty-two with the stroke and the bar, clean of rust; and a field ledger on its face, last and nearest. Behind them a flat case of board under tarred canvas, as long as a man's arm, with a shoulder strap, its lid thrown back and nothing in it. Karis: "a kit for one thing… He came to put iron on an edge." Seln: "They lie in the order that they came out… Nobody packs so. He meant to take them up again." One track in the dust came to it from the door; Seln cannot find it going out.
   749	- **The ledger (flag; changed in repair r1):** limp hide, no bigger than Hesk's book, ruled in faint blue, a page and a half written in a small upright hand, standing, on a knee. A head line of four signs from an index that Seln does not know, and the number; eight distances by the compass points and half-points, "to floor", every one the same; a line headed *Reach, edge to centre.*, gone over twice (its figure is read aloud to Karis and not printed); a few lines on the floor and the stair, not read out; and on the second page the last line and a stroke of the pencil going off toward the edge. **Its eight stations are the old irons:** Karis found an old scaled bar at each of those eight points that morning, within a boot of her turfs. Seln therefore dates the book with the old iron at the stone, **five cold terms**, which is what lets the closing log's "five years ago" stand. **The stroke-and-bar is two visits:** it lay on the unused bar in this man's case five cold terms ago, and somebody set the same mark on the newer bar at the stone "a good deal later"; Seln will put neither a meaning nor a date on it. Seln puts the ledger inside his own coat.
   750	- **Karis's count:** the Ring's figure had had her thumb over it for twelve days; "the cup was under it" and "the cup and the bowl together were over it". The hook with two bars is "close" on two walls, so wide was only the ninth stone. Forty-one at both, "in the courses here", with no forty-second and none wanting; the line between the courses stands outside that count. She had been over the Fallow's eleven strips for four days at a trestle in a barn.
   751	- **The brass on the hide:** she strikes out *guess* beside the ring a day south of Lowmarch, and says that she is going to find out how far wrong a map of days can be along one line.
   752	- **Teague (flag):** the slip is a shingle, nine days on the oak: *Millrace. Three stakes of the weir, gone in the last high water. To be set before the ground shuts. Four marks.* He has a day's posts still to set on the forty-mark fence. He tells his three the true reason at the fork and gives them leave to stay; none does. He sets two stakes and leaves the third, which forfeits the four marks. He once walked eleven cold terms beside a Wind, in another crew, and has worked beside two Embers. His unreported rank was earned on a ledge in the cold.
   753	- **Roles (all unnamed):** the mill's carter; the stooped carter; the ford-keeper at Millrace; the miller and his wife; the miller's man; the father and the small girl; the old woman and her two grandsons; a carter with a wrenched back, a boy with a stye, a woman with a tooth, a sullen man with a brother; the girl at the next boards; the six on the rim.
   754	
   755	### State at movement end (author; governed by the rulings above and by the surviving prose)
   756	**Calendar.** Late afternoon of the **hundred and fifteenth day past the Line**, at the stone on the eastern edge of the Long Stair, in full sun. No season, month or English weekday name is on the page ("the cold term", "frost", "the thaw", "the snow", "a dry month"). Days on the page: 111, 112, 113, 114, 115; in Seln's window, the night of the 112th; in Teague's, the 113th, 114th and the dawn of the 115th. Weather: hard frost every night, ringing roads, ice along Lowmarch's bank; no snow. The next movement opens where this one stops.
   757	
   758	**Where everyone is.** The six at the stone, with two mules (the brown, the crew's; the mill's grey jenny, Oryn's for now), three boxes, and the kit on the turf. Teague and his three have gone back up the ridge toward Millrace. The six from the rim went off north, south and west at dusk on the 114th and have not been seen since.
   759	
   760	**Cael.** Nineteen. **Ten confirmed fragments.** **Deployment since the Line: twenty-three**, by his own log (fourteen on the 113th; nine on the 114th; none on the 115th). **Shadow used twice in all** (the Ring; the Stair), unseen by anybody outside the circle who could carry it; it is shut again. Anchor: looked twice and unbound once. The tenth has now read one other person, with her leave, at one sitting; **he has still read himself once only**, and owes Oryn two more readings on different days and then all of it; she told him "not tonight" on the 113th. What he found in himself is unsaid and unnamed aloud. Body: the right forearm struck (bends on the 115th); a weal across the left palm, taped, unmended; hands warm again. He has said *Noted* twice to Teague. He holds the kit (Brom carries it). Hesk's satchel: the log, the old volume, the notebook, the letters, both of the broker's papers, Pike's lines on bark. Three letters still unanswered.
   761	
   762	**Lira.** Wind; Iron, Rank One. No burst. **Left arm: two marks reopened on the stair wall on the 114th, closed again that night; slung shorter, the hand at the collar-bone; under the order until first snow, and "not going to hold anything else this season".** Right ankle taped, unmended, gone once more on the stair; her tape is used up. She ran two hundred and twenty yards on the released shin. She carried the healer's bow down and held the stair with it for a day without loosing. She found sixty-two stations of an edge with her foot. Her "walk away" is entered, and she went.
   763	
   764	**Brom.** Iron Skin; Copper. **Right shoulder: bolted through from behind on the 114th, mended once that night; the arm bound across his chest; nothing on that side until Oryn says.** He carried the kit up forty steps on the left. He has the healer's bow again, its hook, and **five bolts**. He has quoted Rooke. He lays six bowls.
   765	
   766	**Karis.** Ember; Iron, Rank Three. One point set, on her lamp, outside the stone on the night of the 114th. Holds: **forty sheets** (eleven of the Ring, twenty-nine of the Stair); **forty-one kinds, the same at both**, entered and underlined; **the line between the courses** (not on the Ring; on every wall of the chamber; recurring at ten cords and a piece, two hundred and twenty yards and a stride, the perimeter's radius), shown on the eastern wall's sheets; the Stair's perimeter table (the Fallow's round, to the stride); the surveyor's *Reach, edge to centre* figure, written down from Seln's reading; the leaf of the trials, inside her coat; the calfskin, with *guess* struck out beside the Stair's ring and still standing beside the far south-eastern one; the Ternhall brass, out of its cloth and held over the hide. Her hypothesis is unentered and unsaid. She has said that the floor was carved by somebody who knew the circle's size, and nobody took it up.
   767	
   768	**Seln.** Shadow; Bronze. Unmarked. Angry once: a stock broken across the stone, and "No faces." **The case is on its strap, on him, shut. The kit's ledger is inside his coat.** He has said that he wants a face. He has read the lure, the trap and the kit. He has dated the ledger with the old irons (five cold terms) and has said that the stroke-and-bar stands for two separate visits, the later one undated; he has refused to name the index or the mark, or to say what became of the surveyor.
   769	
   770	**Oryn.** Tide; Iron, Rank Four. **On the round's terms, as a stop:** "when I say lie still, you lie still, and when I say I can't help you, you believe me"; she goes when the round cannot spare her. Nineteen days late at the ford with the oxen by her own sum, "or more"; word sent north by a carter, with where to send for the child. Five mendings in two days (three at Millrace; Brom and Lira at the stone). She refused him at her own bench and offered on the road, the same day ("a different question"). She has been read by him once, short (one hand on her wrist) and long, and has been told what he found in her. She has not forgiven him, and has not been told what he found in himself. She does not know where the ninth came from. Her pannier and rope are on the grey jenny.
   771	
   772	**Teague.** He stood for the five at Lowmarch and sat at "eleventh". He hung the second paper on the nail. He took and forfeited a four-mark slip to be near; he saw one boy, outside the edge, declare a Wind, a fire and a third thing that he cannot name; he said only that he saw a man walk out of the Quiet declaring, and asked nothing. His three walked the ruts by lantern and brought in three crossbows. He held the rim through the 115th and lent a lantern. He still has a day's posts on the forty-mark fence. His crew is unnamed; the healer of no Path has not appeared.
   773	
   774	**The six.** Unseen since dusk on the 114th. No face, name, seal, office or word. They know that a fence failed at its knot on the edge, and that a boy came out of the old road and declared, outside, more than one thing. They saw nothing work inside. They did not touch the boxes.
   775	
   776	**Objects and money.** Board purse: **a hundred and thirty-two earned; a hundred and two in hand**, unchanged. The three hundred lies in Pike's box against the broker's mark, and will not be marked. The Fallow hundred lies there too. **The kit:** case, pacing cord knotted at the fives, stake thirty-two with the stroke and the bar and a bright turned point, mallet; the ledger apart, with Seln. Four crossbows by the stone, strings cut and locks empty, one broken; the rim's recovered shafts in a row beside them (thirteen loosed, by the log of the 114th), useless to the healer's bow. Karis's lamp filled; Teague's lantern returned. Owed still: the ferryman's rope; three replies; Hesk's parcel has not come.
   777	
   778	**Pike's book and the wall.** **Eight lines**; the eighth said twice in the crew's hearing on the 112th. **Eleventh; Teague ten up.** The Stair paper carries five strokes and **HOLD, on Pike's own mark**, said to the room and to be said to the meadow. Pike has said that his book has no column for what the eighth line means.
   779	
   780	**Who knows what.** The mechanism: six. That the fragments answered at the Stair: the six. Which fragments, by Path: the six; where the ninth came from: the five. Teague and his three: a declaration outside the edge, of more than one kind. Pike and the room: that six went in at the Fallow and six came out. The registry's side: as above. Nobody has said why anything answers him inside. Nobody has put a verb between a made floor, an age with no ruler, the same round twice, a circle whose size the carvers knew, and a boy.
   781	
   782	**Open threads.** A bearing through two rings on a calfskin, and a third thing on it. A line between the courses of a wall that keeps a circle's radius, and that the Ring does not carry. A mark set twice, at two visits, that nobody will date. A surveyor who stopped in the middle of a line. An index and a two-stroke mark that Seln will not read. Six people who went off a rim without their bows. Two readings and a telling, owed. A healer nineteen late and counting, with a child whom she cannot hear. An arm that held a stair; an ankle that nobody has mended; a shoulder mended once. A captain who saw three things and asked nothing. A paper with HOLD on it that will never be paid. A fence at forty with a day's posts to set.
