     1	# Chapter 21 — The Drowned Hall
     2	
     3	The bearing was a line on a map for three days and then it was a place, and the place was where the line said it would be, to the stride, and he catalogued that from the last rise before anything it contained, because Karis had walked three days with a straightedge in her coat and had not once said *if*.
     4	
     5	Three days south and east of the Stair, along a road that was a road for a day, a track for a day, and a carter's memory for the third. The kit's bearing, set on the carter's map with the brass straightedge at the Stair's rim and run through the Ring's circle and on, had crossed the carter's third blank at its middle, and Karis had said so at the rim and had said nothing else about it for three days, and on the third afternoon the track came over a low rise into a marsh, and in the marsh's middle stood a hall.
     6	
     7	He catalogued it from the rise. Walls of the grey stone to a man's shoulder and no higher, roofless, a square or near it, twelve strides on a side — a hall the size of a hold's common room, and no larger. Water inside them — the light off it flat and brown, the surface reaching the walls' feet and standing in the doorway where a door had been — and water outside them, in a ring: a marsh, reed and sedge and standing pools, spreading out from the hall's walls in every direction to a line he could not see and did not need to, because he knew its radius before Karis laid a cord on it. A spring had come up through the floor. The hall had drowned in it, from the inside, and the drowning had gone out through the doorway and the walls' feet and made a country of wet ground round the thing, exactly as wide as the Quiet was.
     8	
     9	"Third," Karis said, beside him, and took the straightedge out of her coat and did not open it.
    10	
    11	---
    12	
    13	The line was where Lira said it was, at the marsh's edge where the track left the last dry ground: "Gone," and a stride back, "Back," and Karis's stone in the track's rut, and the six of them stood at it with the mules behind and looked at two hundred meters of marsh with a drowned hall in the middle of it.
    14	
    15	One stake. Seln found it in the reeds ten paces along the line, old, five winters' scale, the registry's mark and a number — thirty-three — and no second stake, and no second mark. He read the ground round it as he read all ground, and went still at the shelf, and said, "Churned."
    16	
    17	It was. The mud was not marsh mud. It was mud that had been *worked* — furrowed, heaved, turned over in ridges the length of a man and the width of a cart's bed, dried at the tops and wet in the troughs, and it lay along the water's edge where the marsh went from wet ground to standing water, and nowhere else, and nowhere drier.
    18	
    19	"Something's moved that from underneath," Seln said. "Not walked on it. Heaved it, from under the water's edge, where the marsh is soft enough to heave. It's never been out. There's no mark of anything on the top of the ridges but the weather." He did not stand up. "It lives in the water, and the water's inside the line. I don't like the stake."
    20	
    21	Nobody asked why. Out at the hall's own lip, where the wall's foot met the water, a second bar of iron stood out of the mud, and it was not straight. Bent — by more than a hand's height, or it would not have shown at a hundred and seventy meters — and bent the way a nail bends when a thing under it pushes up, not the way a nail bends when a thing above it pushes down; Seln said so, and Seln's eyes were what they were. Seln had seen it from the line. Seln had not gone to it.
    22	
    23	"From below," Karis said, with the cord in her hand and not laid.
    24	
    25	"From below," Seln said.
    26	
    27	---
    28	
    29	They spent the afternoon on trials, and he wrote it as one, because Karis would, and because the six of them had walked into two floors on folklore and a stake and had learned twice what that cost. The trials could not be run from the line; it was a hundred and seventy meters out in the marsh and nothing a man could throw would reach the water from it. So they crossed at the stone in the order Seln gave, Oryn checking her hands at the stride and saying "Gone," and went in along the one dry way — a causeway the drowning had left, a stride wide, standing water either side — to the hall's outer wall, and ran the trials from its coping on the marsh side, with the rule already walking with them: nobody stops on anything with water under it. The wall was not the floor; they stood on stone the floor could not reach and did not once go through the doorway. At dusk they went back out along the causeway and over the stone, and Oryn said "Back," and they camped at the line.
    30	
    31	Oryn first, because Oryn had the word, and she gave it at the stone before anyone crossed.
    32	
    33	"Carters won't say it near water," she said. She was on the track's dry end with her hands turned over on her knees and the roll on her back, looking at the hall the way she looked at a thing with a rule in it. "There's a hall on a bearing south of the Stair road with a spring in it, and there's a thing under the spring, and the carters who go that way — there aren't many — call it the wold-wyrm. I've never seen it. Nobody I've had my hands on has seen it whole. I've had a man's leg that had been in its mouth, and he said it came up under him out of a foot of water like the floor had opened, and he never saw a head." She looked at the churned mud. "That's the whole of what the roads know. Big. Under. Comes up. Nobody's seen it whole."
    34	
    35	Karis wrote it as a source, underlined *comes up*, and said, "Then we make it come up where we can see it."
    36	
