    19	The pen stopped. "That's said."
    20	
    21	"That's said," Cael said.
    22	
    23	He read the page upside down across the plank, because he read everything upside down and had never once stopped: nine completed lines under the five names, the last three of them the traverse, the Ring, and the Stair, in Pike's hand, each with its outcome and its price, and the Stair's price column saying *three hundred; taken; not paid*, in the small hand Pike used for a thing that was true and was not going to change. Pike put two fingers flat on the ledger and turned it, with one push, so that it faced across the plank, which he had done twice in a season, and Cael read it the right way up, and it said what it had said.
    24	
    25	Pike did not write a number.
    26	
    27	He had the wall sheet on the plank beside the book — the thirteen crews in the one-armed hand, top to bottom, Teague's four at the top where they had been for three years, and the five names eleventh, where he had put them after the Ring — and he had the pen over it, and he did not put it down, and the room, which had been not-looking at the five of them since the Stair in the way a room does not look at a thing it has decided about, stopped pretending it was not looking at the pen.
    28	
    29	Pike said, to the sheet: "Teague says you need a column I don't have. I've kept this ledger thirty years. First time it's been wrong about a crew by being too small."
