     1	# Chapter 53 — A Map of Days
     2	
     3	The brass came down on the calfskin without a sound, and lay across both rings.
     4	
     5	Karis took her hands away from it. It was a strip no longer than her forearm, and it had come from a shelf at Ternhall, in the bottom of a box, across a Line and a mountain with a herd on it. It lay now on a carter's hide, on the lid of that same box. Its upper end rested on the ring that she had walked on the ninety-sixth day, and its edge passed through the middle of the ring that she had walked that morning.
     6	
     7	"It doesn't reach," said Lira.
     8	
     9	"It need not." Karis set the point of her pencil against the brass at its lower end, and made a dot on the skin; then she slid the strip down along itself with two fingers, a hand's length, until the dot and the lower ring both lay against it again. "A ruler is as long as its owner is patient."
    10	
    11	She did it three times. Cael watched the bright edge go down the hide, past the square beyond the ridge that was a mill, and out over the place where the carter's hill-hatching thinned. All six of them had their heads over the box by then, with the healer a stride and a half behind the rest.
    12	
    13	At the fourth sliding the brass ran in among the strokes of his water.
    14	
    15	They lay thick in that corner, wavy and close, as he had drawn them nowhere else on the skin. In the midst of them was the round of bare hide that Karis had ringed on the ninety-second evening, with a word beside it. The edge went by that ring, on the side toward the corner, and there was the width of a finger between the brass and her pencilled round.
    16	
    17	"It's missed," said Brom.
    18	
    19	"By a finger, at the end of four hands of calfskin, on a map that a man made by counting his suppers." Her voice was small and quite steady, and she had not taken her eyes from the gap. "I said that I should find out how far wrong a map of days could be along one line. That is how far. I think it very handsome of him."
    20	
    21	"Or the ruler's wrong," said Lira.
    22	
    23	"The ruler is brass. It is the two rings under it that I answer for, and I set one where a carter bent his road and the other where a broker's paper said a day and a little aside." She laid a finger beside the third. "This one I drew round a place where he had lifted his hand. He told me, on the evening that he gave me the skin, that he had seen that corner once, from a hill, with water standing in it, and had never had a wheel within a day of it. So I have three things on this lid, each of them a little wrong, and I cannot say by how much."
    24	
    25	"Then move the ring," said Brom. "It's a finger. Who's to know?"
    26	
    27	"I should know. I shall not move a ring to meet a ruler, and I shall not bend a ruler to meet a ring." She took the brass up and wrapped it in its cloth. "We walk the brass. When we come to the place, the ground will say which of them was out."
    28	
    29	Seln had been reading the hide over her shoulder, as he read any paper.
    30	
    31	"His south road lies under your edge for a day's ticks beyond this stone. After that there is a single stroke, which is how he draws a track that he would not take a cart along, and it keeps beside the edge for a day more. Then it stops, and there is nothing under the brass that a wheel made."
    32	
    33	"Then that is the order in which we shall walk it: a road, and a track, and whatever a man remembered from a hill." Karis rolled the skin. "A ruler on a map of days gives me a direction that is good to the breadth of this pencil at the length of my arm, and by the third evening that is a strip of country a mile wide. I am not going to walk a line. I am going to walk a strip, with six pairs of eyes, and look at all of it."
    34	
    35	Cael heard what she had left out of that, and he saw by Lira's face that she had heard it too. A researcher who would not write down what a number counted had said *when we come to the place*, in front of five people, about a ring that she had marked *guess*.
    36	
    37	The healer had listened to the whole of it with the grey mule's rope over her shoulder.
    38	
    39	"Three days, by his ticks?"
    40	
    41	"Three, to the near side of the ring."
    42	
    43	Oryn spread her left hand, and folded the thumb down.
    44	
    45	"I shut that shoulder last night. A mend so deep wants a week of somebody's eye on it, as her arm wanted its fortnight, and I've said in a yard what I think of a healer who spends an hour of herself on a thing and walks off in the week that it's deciding. That's the hundred and twenty-first." A finger followed the thumb. "There's an edge at the end of that brass. I said in a loft where somebody ought to stand when you cross one, and I learned yesterday what it's worth to stand there." Another finger. "And I've sent word north that I'm nineteen, or more. It's the *or more* that I'm spending."
    46	
    47	She looked at what was left of the hand.
    48	
    49	"So I'll see the shoulder through its week, and I'll be at your next edge with my sleeves up. After that I'm the route, and nobody says a word."
    50	
