    55	It was Brom who answered her, and he did it by going to the left-hand box for the bowls. He set out six on the turf between the two irons, as he had on every night since a grey stone on the Fallow; and the healer, who had told a girl at a mill that she would eat out of the sixth and not sit in a place, watched him do it and held her tongue.
    56	
    57	---
    58	
    59	They left the stone at first light on the hundred and sixteenth day, and for three days the country was kinder to them than it had any need to be.
    60	
    61	The frost had gone off in the night. Cael woke to a low grey sky and a smell of wet earth, which he had not smelled for three weeks, and by the middle of the forenoon the ruts were dark and soft where they had rung the day before. The carters' road came back to its line beyond the low ground, as a one-armed man had said that it would, and went on south and east under the foot of the ridge. A salt cart had been along it a week ago, and nothing since.
    62	
    63	Seln went a quarter of a mile in front, where he could be seen against the sky. Karis walked behind him with the hide under her arm, and at every halt she unrolled it on her knee, and laid the brass along it, and looked from the skin to the country and back again. Lira came next, on the whole of her foot, and a road gone soft was no better for her than a road gone hard. The great boot slid in the mud as it had skated on the ice, and her right arm went out, and her left stayed where a knot held it.
    64	
    65	Brom had the brown mule and two of the boxes, and the kit on his own back by its strap. The healer came last with the grey, which carried the third.
    66	
    67	"You're listing," she said.
    68	
    69	"I'm carrying."
    70	
    71	"You're carrying on the one side and walking as if you had two. Let that strap out a hand, and take it across you." She waited while he did it. "That's a week I've given that shoulder. I'd as soon you didn't spend it for me in a morning."
    72	
    73	"It doesn't hurt."
    74	
    75	"It's not meant to hurt. It's meant to be let alone, and those aren't the same thing. Ask her."
    76	
    77	"They're not," said Lira, without turning her head. "It's a deal worse when it stops hurting. You forget that it's there."
    78	
    79	They slept that night in a halting-ring, with a fire of Seln's flint, and Cael sat his watch with Hesk's book shut on his knee. He owed a healer two readings of himself yet, on two different days, and he had not laid a finger on his own wrist since she told him *not tonight* between two bars of iron. He let it stand. She had asked for a night that was only a night; every one since had been the eve of a floor or the morrow of one, and he thought it likely that this was the eve of another. The debt weighed on him a good deal less than it had on a halting-ground above the wash, and he took that for a sign that he had begun to believe her.
    80	
    81	On the second morning the road swung away south toward a hold whose smoke they could see, and the brass did not go with it.
    82	
    83	There was a track where Seln had said there would be: two ruts, grown over, with a tree as thick as Brom's leg standing in one of them. Nobody had driven it in a lifetime. It ran on along the low ground between pale grass and standing pools, and the day was so mild that gnats hung over the water in the afternoon, and a few yellow leaves were still on the silver-backed trees. Brom walked with his coat open.
    84	
    85	"And if there's nothing at the end of this but a pond?" he said, at the noon halt.
    86	
    87	"When we come to it, I shall tell you what is there," said Karis.
    88	
    89	"That's not what I asked."
    90	
    91	"It is the only form of the question that I am able to answer."
    92	
    93	She had the brass on the hide again as she said it. In three days he did not once hear her use the word that Brom had used; she spoke of *the third*, and of *the place*, and of what she would do with her cord when she stood at its edge, as another person might have spoken of a town that she had lived in.
    94	
    95	The track went under the turf on the morning of the hundred and eighteenth.
    96	
    97	It did not end at anything. The ruts grew shallower for a mile, and then there were none, and they were walking on wet grass with the hide for their only road. Karis carried it open before her in both hands. Where the carter's single stroke stopped, his wavy lines began; and inside the hour there was water on either hand of them, black and still, lying in the low places among the reeds exactly as a man had set it down on a calfskin twenty years ago.
    98	
    99	"He saw this," she said. "From that hill to the west, I should think, with the sun behind him. He drew every stroke of it from there, and he never came a step nearer."
   100	
   101	Seln had stopped ahead of them, on the crown of a low rise, with his hands at his sides. He did not lift two fingers. He stood where he was until the five of them had come up beside him and could see what he saw.
   102	
   103	The ground fell away in front of them into a basin as round as a plate. It was full of reeds, the colour of old straw, with black water shining between the stems; and the reeds all stopped at one edge, on every side, as though somebody had gone round them with a pair of shears. From the foot of the rise the old ruts came out of the turf again and ran straight down to that edge. Beyond it something pale and narrow went on across the marsh in the same line, as straight as a ruled stroke.
   104	
   105	It ended at a hall.
   106	
   107	He had no other word for it. Four grey walls stood up out of the water in the middle of the basin, square, and roofless, with the sky in them; they were broken down in some places and whole in others, and through a gap in the nearest he could see water inside that moved, and caught the light. It was the grey of two floors that he had stood on.
   108	
   109	Seln had his hand flat on the breast of his coat, where a surveyor's book lay.
   110	
   111	"Third."
