     1	# Chapter 57 — Four Make an Argument
     2	
     3	It had been the afternoon of the hundred and nineteenth day when Karis sat down at the far fire with three clean leaves before her, and by the dusk of the hundred and twentieth she had risen from them only to sleep and to pace a marsh.
     4	
     5	She was making one leaf three times.
     6	
     7	She had never done such a thing in her life. A leaf was written once, and dated, and laid in its place, and the place was entered; that was what a box was for. But there were three boxes, and she had seen what a mountain could do to a cart. On the seventy-eighth day everything that the five of them owned had gone into a gorge except those three, and they had come off the hill only because a very large person went back for two of them, and a boy broke three ribs over the third. She had counted the loss that night, when she could count again. It had been blankets, and a kettle, and a rope.
     8	
     9	It would not be blankets now. Until the Fallow there had been nothing in those boxes that did not also stand on a shelf somewhere to the west, where she was no longer let in. Since the Fallow there had been a good deal, and since this morning there was a thing that existed nowhere in the world but on the paper in front of her.
    10	
    11	So there would be one leaf in each box, and the first of them in her coat, with a leaf of trials that had a struck numeral on it.
    12	
    13	She wrote small. At the head she set the three places by the names that carters gave them, since she had no better: the Fallow Ring, and the Long Stair, and the hall. Under each went its perimeter, which was sixty-two cords and twenty strides, three times over; and its radius, ten cords and a piece; and the number on its stake. Thirty-one. Thirty-two, on two bars. Thirty-three.
    14	
    15	Then the walls, and here she was careful, since she had been greedy that morning and knew it. She had eleven sheets from the Fallow, where the bands ran round the feet of eleven stones, and twenty-nine from a room under the Stair. She had twelve from the hall. That made fifty-two, and the last twelve were all from one wall, rubbed upside down in an hour, by a person who was being held by the belt.
    16	
    17	She had gone through them twice by the fire, with the cup of barley at her knee. There were thirty-three kinds on those twelve sheets. Every one of the thirty-three was among her forty-one, and there was not a stroke on any sheet that was not. She entered it so: *one wall; thirty-three of the forty-one; none strange; a sample, and no more.* It would have been very easy to write a larger sentence, and she had struck larger sentences out of better people's work.
    18	
    19	There was the other thing, between the courses.
    20	
