| 32:129 | “He had its weight. He had been carrying a pace and a half…” | `books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-11.md:119` — “He got the hound by the loose skin at the back of its neck…” | varied | “The throw” is required; its mechanics are not. Both tellings take the hound behind the skull and at the hind end, remove its rear purchase, turn the throw through Cael's back or hip, make the jaws release because the animal cannot brace, and land it in the south grass. Break the chain by using the lane edge, another person's leverage, or the animal's forward motion; do not begin with the neck-and-hindquarter grip. |
